WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.254 --> 00:00:02.013
- There was a moment of,
I was going unconscious,

00:00:02.013 --> 00:00:04.222
and there was just sort of lack of concern

00:00:04.222 --> 00:00:08.055
about the life they left
laying in that alley.

00:00:10.684 --> 00:00:13.178
- My brother was shot
by an African-American

00:00:13.178 --> 00:00:14.588
when I was a young kid.

00:00:14.588 --> 00:00:18.199
And so I had that fear
of African-Americans.

00:00:18.199 --> 00:00:22.395
Fear of anybody, pretty
much, who wasn't like me.

00:00:22.395 --> 00:00:26.562
And the anger during my
adolescent years and into my 20s

00:00:28.226 --> 00:00:29.667
was just over the top.

00:00:29.667 --> 00:00:32.080
- I just was one of those
kids that never felt

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:35.635
there was ever any issue
or problem with who I was.

00:00:35.635 --> 00:00:39.647
It wasn't until I was 13
and sort of made that clear.

00:00:39.647 --> 00:00:42.075
And the reaction of my family was,

00:00:42.075 --> 00:00:43.232
I was thrown out.

00:00:43.232 --> 00:00:47.399
- For me, you know, a lot of
times, to escape the suburbs,

00:00:48.753 --> 00:00:51.910
we were being picked on by the
jocks or the football players

00:00:51.910 --> 00:00:54.190
or the popular kids or
whatever we, you know,

00:00:54.190 --> 00:00:58.900
in the late 70's, early 1980's,
punk rockers were freaks.

00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:02.191
We would go into Hollywood
and we were on the rampage.

00:01:02.191 --> 00:01:04.839
The police didn't like
us, nobody liked us.

00:01:04.839 --> 00:01:07.506
I liked that negative attention.

00:01:08.461 --> 00:01:11.447
Ooh, these people are weird,
these people are scary.

00:01:11.447 --> 00:01:15.300
After concerts, we would hang
out at this hot dog stand

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:16.875
called Oaky Dogs.

00:01:16.875 --> 00:01:21.308
There were little pockets
of individual groups

00:01:21.308 --> 00:01:23.308
within the larger tribe,

00:01:24.148 --> 00:01:27.217
who were more violent than others.

00:01:27.217 --> 00:01:29.286
- When my mom threw me out,

00:01:29.286 --> 00:01:31.562
because of the fact that I was gay,

00:01:31.562 --> 00:01:33.177
I figured well, I'll show her.

00:01:33.177 --> 00:01:35.266
I'm gonna go to Hollywood,
become a successful actor,

00:01:35.266 --> 00:01:37.219
and then I'll just be like hey, whatever.

00:01:37.219 --> 00:01:39.685
I ended up, you know, living in a park

00:01:39.685 --> 00:01:41.425
directly across the street from

00:01:41.425 --> 00:01:45.352
the very same hamburger stand that Tim and

00:01:45.352 --> 00:01:49.246
a lot of his crew hung out at as well.

00:01:49.246 --> 00:01:51.837
Except that I had never
seen them there until

00:01:51.837 --> 00:01:53.983
that one night that I was there,

00:01:53.983 --> 00:01:56.106
hanging out like I normally
do, playing video games.

00:01:56.106 --> 00:01:59.135
So as I looked up and saw them sort of,

00:01:59.135 --> 00:02:00.843
kinda coming across the street,

00:02:00.843 --> 00:02:02.617
being very loud and boisterous,

00:02:02.617 --> 00:02:04.515
there was absolutely no concern.

00:02:04.515 --> 00:02:07.680
It wasn't until, whether
it was Tim or someone else,

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:08.562
yelled--

00:02:08.562 --> 00:02:10.369
- "Let's kill the faggots!"

00:02:10.369 --> 00:02:12.159
- And what ended up
happening is, all that energy

00:02:12.159 --> 00:02:15.337
from all of, however many there were,

00:02:15.337 --> 00:02:17.254
was, descended upon me.

00:02:18.096 --> 00:02:20.390
In what was a very brutal attack.

00:02:20.390 --> 00:02:24.517
- Matthew, um, was being
kicked on the ground

00:02:24.517 --> 00:02:26.020
and he was still moving around.

00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:27.849
And there was like, several people.

00:02:27.849 --> 00:02:30.411
I came up to them, I
said, "What's," you know,

00:02:30.411 --> 00:02:32.462
"come on, do what you're supposed to do.

00:02:32.462 --> 00:02:34.012
"What's takin' so long?"

00:02:34.012 --> 00:02:36.596
And I'd actually kicked
Matthew in the head.

00:02:36.596 --> 00:02:38.590
And there was high-fives,
and then it was like,

00:02:38.590 --> 00:02:40.092
there was a girl with us who screamed,

00:02:40.092 --> 00:02:41.558
"You guys are so messed up!"

00:02:41.558 --> 00:02:43.209
And then we said "Oh wow," you know,

00:02:43.209 --> 00:02:45.717
"Maybe we killed this guy."

00:02:45.717 --> 00:02:47.558
And so we got in our cars and we left.

00:02:47.558 --> 00:02:50.627
- And when I come to, nobody was there.

00:02:50.627 --> 00:02:51.937
Everybody had left the hamburger stand.

00:02:51.937 --> 00:02:53.440
Nobody was there putting
their hand on my shoulder,

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:54.655
nobody was saying, "I'm
calling the police."

00:02:54.655 --> 00:02:56.232
Nobody was tellin' me
it was gonna be okay.

00:02:56.232 --> 00:02:58.202
Nobody was like, "I'll
take you to the hospital,"

00:02:58.202 --> 00:02:59.166
throwing me in the car.

00:02:59.166 --> 00:03:02.067
There's a moment of, this violent attack,

00:03:02.067 --> 00:03:04.840
based in hatred and fear, and then,

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:07.090
absolutely being invisible.

00:03:11.297 --> 00:03:15.407
There was murder that
took place in this country

00:03:15.407 --> 00:03:19.335
that, to me, like, ripped a
hole in the soul of this country

00:03:19.335 --> 00:03:21.835
and brought a nation together.

00:03:26.980 --> 00:03:28.106
The connection I have with him

00:03:28.106 --> 00:03:30.661
is that I survived, and he didn't.

00:03:30.661 --> 00:03:32.415
And I remember a client saying to me,

00:03:32.415 --> 00:03:35.065
"If you're so bothered by it,
what have you done about it?"

00:03:35.065 --> 00:03:37.055
I said, "Well, nothing, I
don't know what I can do."

00:03:37.055 --> 00:03:39.019
And she goes, "Well then stop
complaining to me about it.

00:03:39.019 --> 00:03:41.243
"You can either sit
back and be complacent,

00:03:41.243 --> 00:03:43.134
"and accept what you see in the news,

00:03:43.134 --> 00:03:46.311
"or you can take part,
and be something that

00:03:46.311 --> 00:03:47.897
"is an instrument of change."

00:03:47.897 --> 00:03:51.712
And I ended up, believe it
or not, leaving that career.

00:03:51.712 --> 00:03:54.197
And moving on to the Museum
of Tolerance in Los Angeles,

00:03:54.197 --> 00:03:55.745
just to volunteer, and ended up becoming

00:03:55.745 --> 00:03:57.426
the manager of that museum.

00:03:57.426 --> 00:04:01.284
- I actually ended up
getting involved with a

00:04:01.284 --> 00:04:04.826
rather large organized
national hate group.

00:04:04.826 --> 00:04:08.743
Moved up the ranks, and
then I met a young lady

00:04:09.716 --> 00:04:11.573
and had a child.

00:04:11.573 --> 00:04:13.872
And things started to change.

00:04:13.872 --> 00:04:16.471
Eventually, I did make it out

00:04:16.471 --> 00:04:18.262
of the white racialist movement.

00:04:18.262 --> 00:04:20.426
I said that I was moving
out of Southern California,

00:04:20.426 --> 00:04:22.652
I was gonna get out of the
cesspool of Los Angeles,

00:04:22.652 --> 00:04:24.966
raise my family in a
whiter, brighter world.

00:04:24.966 --> 00:04:29.582
And was no longer compatible
with my son's mother.

00:04:29.582 --> 00:04:32.856
I was watching my son being influenced

00:04:32.856 --> 00:04:36.616
by the politics and by the propaganda.

00:04:36.616 --> 00:04:40.022
And I ended up marrying a Jewish woman,

00:04:40.022 --> 00:04:41.463
moved back to southern California,

00:04:41.463 --> 00:04:45.296
and went to go see TJ
Liden speak at a church.

00:04:46.829 --> 00:04:50.306
And he said that he was
leaving the Museum of Tolerance

00:04:50.306 --> 00:04:52.818
to go do his own thing.

00:04:52.818 --> 00:04:55.318
And I asked him, "Do you think

00:04:56.649 --> 00:05:00.135
"they would let me, you know, take over?"

00:05:00.135 --> 00:05:02.376
He gave me a card, I called the museum,

00:05:02.376 --> 00:05:06.543
and I've been there for
over a decade as a result.

00:05:08.465 --> 00:05:11.048
And that's where I met Matthew.

00:05:13.681 --> 00:05:16.030
- A teacher called, she
had a group of students

00:05:16.030 --> 00:05:17.189
that she was bringing, and they wanted

00:05:17.189 --> 00:05:18.987
a powerful speaker to address.

00:05:18.987 --> 00:05:22.287
I invited Tim, or asked
him to go to have coffee,

00:05:22.287 --> 00:05:24.082
so I could ask him, get his opinion.

00:05:24.082 --> 00:05:25.327
Because the issue happened to be

00:05:25.327 --> 00:05:28.321
dealing with white supremacy
in this specific community

00:05:28.321 --> 00:05:30.196
and at this school.

00:05:30.196 --> 00:05:32.570
So what ended up happening
is, Tim was talking to me,

00:05:32.570 --> 00:05:34.064
completely off-topic,
I was like, you know,

00:05:34.064 --> 00:05:35.709
"What part of southern
California did you grow up in?

00:05:35.709 --> 00:05:37.464
"You know, I've never asked you that."

00:05:37.464 --> 00:05:38.508
And the subject came up about

00:05:38.508 --> 00:05:40.624
the hamburger stand, Oaky Dogs.

00:05:40.624 --> 00:05:42.303
It comes out that we both realized,

00:05:42.303 --> 00:05:44.136
almost within seconds of each other,

00:05:44.136 --> 00:05:45.822
who the other one was talking to.

00:05:45.822 --> 00:05:46.958
There was a surge of emotion,

00:05:46.958 --> 00:05:48.084
but not the ones you would think.

00:05:48.084 --> 00:05:49.779
It wasn't anger, it wasn't like

00:05:49.779 --> 00:05:51.426
I wanted to throw something at him,

00:05:51.426 --> 00:05:54.205
there was just a surge of emotions of,

00:05:54.205 --> 00:05:57.372
there was almost a fear, I was scared,

00:05:58.531 --> 00:06:01.291
I was nervous, and the
only thing I knew how to do

00:06:01.291 --> 00:06:02.502
was to leave.

00:06:02.502 --> 00:06:06.738
My best thinking was,
alright, I'm going to

00:06:06.738 --> 00:06:09.119
try to have conversations with Tim.

00:06:09.119 --> 00:06:11.422
Really, the answer I wanted was why?

00:06:11.422 --> 00:06:15.478
- I went over and over and
over and over in my mind

00:06:15.478 --> 00:06:18.340
trying to minimize my part.

00:06:18.340 --> 00:06:20.340
Trying to blame Matthew.

00:06:22.670 --> 00:06:25.807
Trying to blame, you know,
the people I was with,

00:06:25.807 --> 00:06:27.956
well somebody said to
do it, or you know, just

00:06:27.956 --> 00:06:29.732
tryin' to come up with an excuse.

00:06:29.732 --> 00:06:32.675
And at the end of the
day, there is no excuse.

00:06:32.675 --> 00:06:36.646
You know I wasn't planning on
apologizing at that moment,

00:06:36.646 --> 00:06:38.531
it just sorta came out.

00:06:38.531 --> 00:06:41.031
We were asked by the museum to

00:06:41.955 --> 00:06:44.164
start doing presentations together.

00:06:44.164 --> 00:06:46.398
- My first thought was, well, no.

00:06:46.398 --> 00:06:47.874
I haven't processed this enough to

00:06:47.874 --> 00:06:51.136
sit on a stage in a
room full of 300 people.

00:06:51.136 --> 00:06:53.149
There was still a part of me that saw

00:06:53.149 --> 00:06:55.848
the Tim I knew the eight months
before realizing who he was

00:06:55.848 --> 00:06:57.991
and yes he was this
incredible person who had

00:06:57.991 --> 00:07:00.093
changed his life, and I
have always admired people

00:07:00.093 --> 00:07:01.445
who are able to do that.

00:07:01.445 --> 00:07:03.979
Especially if you're
in so deep and then you

00:07:03.979 --> 00:07:05.827
take the courage and the stand.

00:07:05.827 --> 00:07:07.349
The level of honesty and willingness

00:07:07.349 --> 00:07:09.743
to share and spend time with me was a

00:07:09.743 --> 00:07:12.437
huge part of me being able to let go,

00:07:12.437 --> 00:07:13.354
not forget,

00:07:14.539 --> 00:07:17.143
and be able to see him as
a person and a human being

00:07:17.143 --> 00:07:18.792
and then being able to forgive him

00:07:18.792 --> 00:07:20.262
completely and entirely.

00:07:20.262 --> 00:07:23.007
And it never was a friendship
of, let's go to the movies,

00:07:23.007 --> 00:07:25.455
let's have dinner, let's hang
out with each other's family.

00:07:25.455 --> 00:07:29.243
It was more of, we shared a common bond,

00:07:29.243 --> 00:07:31.086
and as difficult as it can be at times,

00:07:31.086 --> 00:07:32.602
the common goal is to share this story

00:07:32.602 --> 00:07:35.219
in order to reach out and reach people.

00:07:35.219 --> 00:07:36.405
That change is possible.

00:07:36.405 --> 00:07:38.935
Not only is change possible,
but forgiveness is possible,

00:07:38.935 --> 00:07:40.228
healing is possible.

00:07:40.228 --> 00:07:43.022
- The world, but especially this nation,

00:07:43.022 --> 00:07:46.772
it has this wound that
has never been healed.

00:07:49.511 --> 00:07:52.919
And recent activities that have happened,

00:07:52.919 --> 00:07:54.780
it's like tearing that wound open

00:07:54.780 --> 00:07:56.360
and sticking your finger in it.

00:07:56.360 --> 00:07:59.777
And until that wound can be truly healed,

00:08:01.915 --> 00:08:03.083
through knowing one another,

00:08:03.083 --> 00:08:04.786
really, really knowing one another,

00:08:04.786 --> 00:08:07.451
not oh, I have a black friend
or I have a white friend,

00:08:07.451 --> 00:08:09.082
do you know them?

00:08:09.082 --> 00:08:10.334
Do you know their middle name?

00:08:10.334 --> 00:08:11.490
Do you know their parents?

00:08:11.490 --> 00:08:13.284
Have you ever been in their house?

00:08:13.284 --> 00:08:14.867
- I do believe that

00:08:16.526 --> 00:08:20.961
it is progressing forward
and it is becoming better.

00:08:20.961 --> 00:08:24.645
I just think it's taking
much longer than it needs to.

00:08:24.645 --> 00:08:28.645
- If Matthew and I can
heal through forgiveness,

00:08:30.813 --> 00:08:33.646
I think other people can as well.

